le, if the W5 and W5 form a pair, and the W7, B1, B3 are singles. That's not valid. So maybe Penging W3 is not the right move here. \n\nAlternatively, if I Pass, I can keep the current hand and see if I can draw or discard to form a better hand. My current hand is [W3, W3, W5, W5, W7, T1, T2, T3, B1, B3, F1, F1, F1]. Wait, no, the original hand is [W3:2; W5:2; W7:1; T1:1; T2:1; T3:1; B1:1; B3:1]. Plus the meld of F1, F1, F1. So total tiles: 2+2+1+1+1+1+1+1 + 3 = 12 tiles? Wait, no. The hand tiles are the ones listed, which sum to 2+2+1+1+1+1+1+1 = 9 tiles. Plus the meld of F1, which is 3 tiles. Total 12 tiles. So I need to draw one more tile to reach 14. \n\nIf I don't Peng W3, then I have 12 tiles in hand (9 from the listed, plus the 3 in the meld). So I need to draw one more. If I draw a tile that can help form a meld. For example, if I draw W6, then I can form a Chow of W5-6-7. Or if I draw B2, then B1-2-3 as a Chow. \n\nBut the unshown tiles include B6:1, B7:3, etc. So B2 is still available. If I draw B2, then I can form a Chow of B1-2-3, which would add another meld. Then my hand would have two Pungs (F1 and maybe another), two Chows (T1-2-3 and B1-2-3), and the remaining tiles would be W5, W5, W7. That could form a pair of W5 and a single W7, but that's not valid. Wait, no. If I have two Chows and two Pungs, that's four melds, and the remaining two W5 and W7 would need to form a pair. But two W5s can be a pair, and W7 is a single, which is not allowed. So that's not a valid hand. \n\nAlternatively, if I have two Chows (T1-2-3 and B1-2-3), one Pung (F1), and then the W5, W5, W7. If I can form a Pung of W5 (but I have two, need one more). Not possible. \n\nSo maybe the best path is to Peng W3 to get a Pung, and then see if the remaining tiles can form a valid hand. But as discussed earlier, after Penging W3, the remaining tiles are W5, W5, W7, B1, B3. That's five tiles. To form a fourth meld and a pair. Maybe the W5, W5 as a pair, and W7, B1, B3 as a single. Not